  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through Friday)\nIssued at 1220 AM MST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nQuieter weather returns early next week as we transition to more\nzonal flow aloft. However, a weak shortwave passing through on\nMonday will bring breezy westerly winds during the afternoon. \n\nThe main story next week will be what looks to be an impactful \nclosed low traversing across the Desert Southwest during the \nTuesday-Thursday timeframe. There is growing agreement among \nlong-term model guidance and ensembles on the development of this \nlow. Impacts look to begin on Tuesday as increasing southwesterly \nflow ahead of the system will bring more breezy conditions to the \nregion, followed by increasing precipitation chances by Tuesday\nnight/Wednesday as the system makes its way inland. Current \nguidance favors more widespread rain due to warmer conditions \nahead of the system. However, mountain snow and a mix of rain and \nsnow will remain possible across western, central, and northern \nhigh terrain beginning Tuesday night and continuing into Thursday.\nSnow levels and precipitation totals will depend on the system's \neventual track and moisture availability. Precipitation will \ngradually taper off from west to east Thursday into Friday as the \nsystem exits. Although, uncertainty remains regarding the timing \nof departure. Temperatures will trend cooler behind this system.",
